# The order-nine clone of equation 692 and its three maximal subclones

`692` is `x = (y ◇ x) ◇ (x ◇ (z ◇ y))`.  Its branch-2 models are extensions of an idempotent-free
semisymmetric quotient by a cyclic group, and the smallest interesting one is `Magma.shift692`
below: nine elements, affine over `(ℤ/3)²` with `x ◇ y = P x + (I - P) y + ρ` for
`P = ![![2, 2], ![0, 2]]` and `ρ = (0, 1)`.  Its binary clone is exactly the `81` affine maps
`P' x + (I - P') y + ρ'` with `P'` upper triangular and scalar on the diagonal.

That clone has exactly three maximal proper subclones, of sizes `54`, `27` and `27`, and their
union is exactly the `69` members that fail to generate `◇` back.  Each of the three is cut out by
a generic shift invariant of `Definability/CloneShift.lean` at one of the two translations
`Magma.shift692Sigma` (add `(1, 0)`) and `Magma.shift692Tau` (add `(0, 1)`):

* `Magma.SplitShift shift692Sigma` — the `54` maps with `P' ∈ {0, I} + ⟨N⟩`;
* `Magma.RespShift shift692Tau` — the `27` maps with `P'` scalar;
* `Magma.DiagShift shift692Sigma` — the `27` maps with `ρ' ∈ ⟨(1, 0)⟩`.

So a target realised only by members of that union is not term-structural from `692`, over finite
magmas and hence over all of them.  That is every target below.

The clone is carried as ASCII, as in `Definability/CloneBig.lean`: `Magma.shift692Data` holds `81`
blocks of `81` digits `0`-`8` and `Magma.shift692Comp` holds each composite index as two base-`9`
digits.  The clone-family check is `81³` equations and goes through `native_decide`, so everything
below additionally depends on `Lean.ofReduceBool`.

This file is generated by `gen692ts.py`.
-/

